  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and fairly muggy today and Friday. Not \nquite as hot over the weekend, but still very warm and muggy.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nRemaining hot today and Friday, with high temperatures near or\njust a hair warmer than what we had on Wednesday. Highs today\nand Friday will be around 90, with only a bit of relief along\nthe immediate coast. Staying fairly warm at night too. Today \nwill be fairly muggy still, but Friday we will see the humidity \nkick up a notch with dewpoints up around 70F. This will push \nheat indices to around heat advisory criteria of 95F or a bit \nhotter. Not ready to issue the advisory yet, but the next shift \nwill need to consider, especially just inland from the immediate\ncoast and north to Dover-Foxcroft and Lincoln. Light winds the \nnext few days will make things feel worse.\n\nOver the weekend, temperatures will be not quite as hot mainly\ndue to increased clouds. That said, it will still easily be\nwarmer than average, with nighttime temperatures staying\nespecially mild. And it will still be muggy over the weekend.\nSome uncertainty in temperatures over the weekend as just how\nwarm it gets will depend on the amount of clouds and\nshowers/storms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some strong storms are possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system approaches from the northwest, moving into\nNew Brunswick. Associate front is continuing to trend slower, \nas it approaches Friday night, through Maine by Sunday. General \ntrends is pointing towards the heavier rainfall falling on \nSaturday. There is a good amount of instability ahead of the \ncold front. NAM shows around 1,500 - 2,000 J/kg of CAPE, good \nlow level lapse rates (roughly 8C/km), and plenty of \nprecipitable water for Saturday afternoon into evening, though \nshear is low. Soundings are moist, with classic long and skinny \nCAPE throughout most of northern and eastern Maine. PWAT values \naround 1.8, with some models hinting at almost 2.0\" on Saturday,\nwhich is around the 90th percentile, approaching the 1.9 \ndaytime maximum value Friday evening, and the 1.7\" record \nSaturday. Keeping the threat of thunder inland, as a seabreeze \noff of the cool Gulf of Maine waters should stabilize and keep \ndaytime high temperatures on the lower side along Downeast Maine\ncoastline on both days. Storm motions based on Corfidi vectors \nseems to be moving fast enough to keep flash flooding at bay, \nbut will need to see how this evolves as the regional models and\nCAMs pick up on the system and preexisting environment. Bottom \nline up front: Saturday seems to be more favorable for heavier \nrainfall, and more expansive thunderstorms.",
